WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.468
-One of my favorite things
about the book is --

00:00:02.468 --> 00:00:04.370
and your tenure now at "SNL" --

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.840
It has been so long,
and I had a great time

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:07.873
writing with you over the years.

00:00:07.873 --> 00:00:09.175
And I'm such a fan
of your writing.

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:10.943
And you talk about a lot
about of your favorite sketches.

00:00:10.943 --> 00:00:14.247
You have created
some real indelible characters.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:17.250
Drunk Uncle,
one of my all-time favorites.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.686
And I should note you created
two with Bobby Moynihan.

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:23.155
The other being Anthony
Crispino, Second Hand News.

00:00:23.155 --> 00:00:25.725
-Second Hand News, yeah.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:27.693
-Did you come up with
Drunk Uncle first

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:31.831
or did you see Bobby and think
that he would be good

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.866
at being a drunk uncle?

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:36.268
He's obviously perfect at it.

00:00:36.268 --> 00:00:39.639
-He's like a deep -- It's one of
the best acting performances

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:42.041
you'll see on our show
'cause it's live.

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:44.910
And he's finding all these
different registers so quickly

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.946
and all these
different emotions.

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:49.748
I had come up with the concept
of Second Hand News.

00:00:49.748 --> 00:00:52.151
And I was like,
"What about Second Hand News?"

00:00:52.151 --> 00:00:54.387
And then he had the voice.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.989
He just had the like,
"Yeah, I don't know about that."

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:59.959
Like that kind of -- which is
a pure performance thing

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:01.560
that gave it
a whole other dimension.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.863
With Drunk Uncle, he came to me
and was like,

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.265
"Two words -- drunk uncle.

00:01:06.265 --> 00:01:08.468
I don't know what it is.
Is there something?"

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:10.002
And I was like, "I think there's
something there."

00:01:10.002 --> 00:01:13.072
And then figuring out, like,
you know, what the hell it was.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.608
But it was -- He was just like,

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.677
"I just thought
that was a funny phrase."

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:21.581
And the weird thing is when you
do that sketch, you know,

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:24.050
Bobby and I both have uncles.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.620
So, of course,
their first question is,

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.556
"Now, is this based on me
in some way?

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:32.925
I know I kind of
go for it on Thanksgiving.

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:35.094
But I can hold back most days."

00:01:35.094 --> 00:01:38.597
And so the first question's
kind of like, "Are we okay?"

00:01:38.597 --> 00:01:41.267
-From your uncles. Yeah.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:47.606
You also wrote Cecily Strong's
"the girl you wish you hadn't

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:49.375
started a conversation with
at a party."

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:50.810
I think I said it right.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.145
It's maybe the longest character
name in the history of "SNL."

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:56.515
-I think almost no one,
even fans of the character,

00:01:56.515 --> 00:01:58.551
know what the character's
called.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:01.954
I think Michael Che said
that his mom calls it

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:03.423
"the girl who's always wrong."

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:04.523
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:06.759
-That's not bad.
-Something like that.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.462
And I was like, "Yeah, that's
pretty -- that's maybe better."

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:12.431
-That was one of the more --
I mean, I think people forget --

00:02:12.431 --> 00:02:14.867
I think that was the first thing
Cecily did on the show.

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:17.903
And it's one of the most,
like, bravura performances

00:02:17.903 --> 00:02:19.438
I've ever seen.

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:21.841
-I remember in --
I write about it in the book,

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:24.910
but I remember being with you
afterwards.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:29.215
Like, when you got offstage and
you had just done it with her.

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:30.616
Like, been out there

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:32.885
and just seen the character
for the first time.

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:35.288
And we had a moment afterwards

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:39.758
where we were both kind of
in awe of her performance

00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:42.828
'cause you just don't see
someone who's brand new

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:45.631
at the show hit it like --

00:02:45.631 --> 00:02:47.600
just knock it
out of the ballpark

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.870
in that specific,
very fine-tuned way.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.105
And the confidence --
-Yeah.

00:02:53.105 --> 00:02:54.407
-It was really special.

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:55.774
-I think there was a moment

00:02:55.774 --> 00:02:58.744
where she glanced
in her purse at a phone.

00:02:58.744 --> 00:03:03.482
There was some sort of physical
moment that I just thought,

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:07.453
"Who can also do this?"

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:11.424
-Yeah. Own a small physical
movement in the middle of

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:13.659
this big character piece
on your first --

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:15.495
one of your first shows
at "SNL."

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:16.729
And the move --
Yeah, you're right.

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.297
I remember she looked
and put the phone --

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:20.866
and just with perfect precision
and ease.

00:03:20.866 --> 00:03:23.469
-Yeah.
It was like, oh, there --

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.905
She threw a perfect game
her first time on the mound.

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:27.974
It was amazing.
-Yes, it really is.

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:31.044
I think that's
the right analogy.

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:35.013
Like, it was like throwing
a perfect game your first game.

00:03:35.013 --> 00:03:38.551
-It's such a fun book.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.452
But that is one of my favorite
things about it,

00:03:40.452 --> 00:03:42.454
is over the years on this show,

00:03:42.454 --> 00:03:44.957
and again, I don't think
a ton of people know this.

00:03:44.957 --> 00:03:48.260
You've done such a great job of
identifying what's really funny

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.297
about cast members
and writing to that voice.

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:53.065
And I think
it's the most important thing

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:54.834
for a writer on that show.

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:56.669
And you're just so good at it,
buddy.

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:59.772
-I mean, I learned
a lot from you.

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:02.375
Like so many people who wrote
at the show in the time

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:05.344
when you were head writer,
you helped me tremendously.

00:04:05.344 --> 00:04:08.514
And I learned about writing
for people from you.

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:10.716
And you would write a scene

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:13.119
where basically
every cast member had a part.

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.254
And it would be
their funniest character

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:17.656
that they never got on the show
in some other way,

00:04:17.656 --> 00:04:21.561
like Fred's Mets character,
where he just --

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:23.763
I remember one sketch you wrote
that was about people

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.898
brainstorming themes for a prom.

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:29.935
And Fred looks like
he's a 45-year-old man

00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:31.871
with a mustache
in a Mets' jacket

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:35.407
at a high school prom
theme meeting who is just like,

00:04:35.407 --> 00:04:37.409
"What about the Mets?

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:39.344
The Mets are pretty cool."

00:04:39.344 --> 00:04:41.881
It was like a throw-away thing
he had done in the office

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:44.449
and you made it a starring part
in this sketch.

00:04:44.449 --> 00:04:45.985
You were great at it.

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:49.555
-I think we both
would admit to the fact

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:51.691
that we have been incredibly
lucky in our careers

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:54.460
to be surrounded by performers
that elevate every word

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.195
you would ever put on the page.
-Yes.

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:59.231
And that is -- that's where you
learn -- I certainly --

00:04:59.231 --> 00:05:02.301
you learn your own limitations
and then you see someone else

00:05:02.301 --> 00:05:03.969
that can do these things
that you cannot.

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:06.772
And you're, like, "Ooh, that's
exciting to write for that --

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:09.041
write for that person's voice"

00:05:09.041 --> 00:05:12.178
and get to do things
that you can't do yourself.

00:05:12.178 --> 00:05:15.180
-Well, buddy, obviously nobody
knows what the future holds,

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.084
but I'm looking forward
to hopefully the fall

00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:19.952
and "SNL" and you at the desk.

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:22.755
And congrats on the book, and
it's always the best to see you.

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:24.022
-Thank you.
It is wonderful to see you.

00:05:24.022 --> 00:05:27.560
I was trying to figure out
some elaborate scam

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.896
when you had an attic door still

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:33.399
of who I could hire
to be in there and jump out

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:35.367
halfway through and scare you.

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:36.702
[ Laughs ]

00:05:36.702 --> 00:05:40.673
But it turns out it's hard
to hire street performers

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:43.342
who will travel to remote places

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:47.480
during a quarantine
to scare people.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.516
-To violate pandemic rules
for the purposes of a laugh?

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:55.054
-And then also hard to convince
wives to let a stranger

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:58.491
hide in a crawl space
for a day before a show.

00:05:58.491 --> 00:06:00.960
-Oh, no, no.
My wife loves all your ideas.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.694
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:02.694 --> 00:06:05.231
They love -- All of our wives
love your ideas the most.

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:08.734
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:10.135
-Alright. I love you, buddy.

00:06:10.135 --> 00:06:11.371
-I love you.
It's great to see you.

